<p>On August 21, 2026, Fortune <a href="https://fortune.com/2026/08/21/exclusive-amazon-quietly-hiked-prices-echo-fire-tv-kindle-eero-significant-increases-memory-costs/" rel="noopener">broke the news</a> that Amazon had quietly raised prices across its device lineup &mdash; not just Kindles, but every Echo, Fire TV and eero product. The full table hit Verge, Engadget, Mashable, and Tom's Guide within hours. The numbers were worse than the original Kindle-only story suggested: the 5th-generation Echo Dot jumped from $49.99 to $79.99, an increase of roughly 60%. The base 16GB Kindle climbed from $109.99 to $149.99, a 36% jump on the device that was supposed to be the budget option.</p>

<p>If you only read EPUB files, the immediate effect on your reading life is small &mdash; the Kindle Paperwhite 16GB "only" went from $159.99 to $199.99, and your existing device still works. But the deeper story, the <em>why</em>, is what every reader who plans to buy hardware in 2026 should understand. This is a <a href="https://www.implicator.ai/amazon-device-prices-60-percent-memory-costs/" rel="noopener">"memory crisis"</a> driven by AI data centers, and it is reshaping what it costs to own reading hardware, smart speakers, and the streaming sticks that power most TVs.</p>

<h2 id="what-actually-changed">The Full List of What Changed on August 21</h2>

<p>Amazon did not issue a press release. The story broke through Fortune's reporting on an internal memo, with Amazon spokesperson Kristy Schmidt confirming the move in a written statement:</p>

<blockquote>
<p>"The consumer electronics industry is facing significant increases in memory and storage component costs. After absorbing these increases for as long as we could, we recently adjusted pricing across our product lines. Our approach has always been to offer great products at accessible prices, and that commitment hasn't changed."</p>
</blockquote>

